<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>SshWarden.Boltway</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Its own project, and that separation is the point of docs/DESIGN.md §6.2: a deployment running
    static tokens must not be made to pull an OAuth 2.1 authorization server's client libraries in
    behind SshWarden.Mcp. Referencing this assembly is the deployment saying it wants them.

    Boltway.ResourceServer and Boltway.OAuth.Net rather than Boltway.Mcp, which is what section 6.2
    names. The departure is deliberate: Boltway.Mcp's own contribution is a connector-caller layer
    and a per-tool policy seam, and SshWarden built both in step 2 - taking Boltway.Mcp would put a
    second tool gate beside the one that already decides, with nothing saying which wins. What this
    actually needs from Boltway is the token validation, the RFC 9728 metadata and the JWKS source,
    and those are in the two packages below.

    Pinned exactly, never a floating range: section 6.2 again, and a range would let a consumer's
    restore pick a version this was never built against.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../SshWarden.Mcp/SshWarden.Mcp.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Boltway.ResourceServer" />
    <PackageReference Include="Boltway.OAuth.Net" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
